WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:03.870
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:10.599
Through the power of computer graphics research
works, today, it is possible to simulate honey

00:00:10.599 --> 00:00:18.590
coiling, water flow with debris, or even get
a neural network to look at these simulations

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:21.070
and learn how to continue them!

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:27.190
Now, if we look under the hood, we see that
not all, but many of these simulations contain

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:28.860
particles.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:33.710
And our task is to simulate the pressure,
velocity, and other physical quantities for

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:39.789
these particles and create a surface where
we can watch the evolution of their movement.

00:00:39.789 --> 00:00:43.379
Once again, the simulations are typically
based on particles.

00:00:43.379 --> 00:00:46.079
But not this new technique.

00:00:46.079 --> 00:00:47.079
Look.

00:00:47.079 --> 00:00:52.309
It takes a coarse simulation, well, this one
is not too exciting.

00:00:52.309 --> 00:00:55.109
So why are we looking at this?

00:00:55.109 --> 00:00:57.579
Well, look!

00:00:57.579 --> 00:00:58.820
Whoa!

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:03.100
The new method can add these crispy, high-frequency
details to it.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:07.400
And the result is an absolutely beautiful
simulation.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:11.870
And it does not use millions and millions
of particles to get this done.

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:15.900
In fact, it does not use any particles at
all!

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:19.040
Instead, it uses wave curves.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:24.350
These are curve-shaped wave-packets that can
enrich a coarse wave simulation and improve

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:29.140
it a great deal to create a really detailed,
crisp output.

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:34.540
And it gets even better, because these wave
curves can be applied as a simple post processing

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:35.660
step.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:40.490
What this means is that the workflow what
you saw here really works like that.

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:46.100
When we have a coarse simulation that is already
done, and we are not happy with it, with many

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:51.720
other existing methods, it is time to simulate
the whole thing again from scratch, but not

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:53.130
here.

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:58.480
With this one, we can just add all this detail
to an already existing simulation.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:01:59.480
Wow.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:00.610
Loving it.

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:05.700
Note that the surface of the fluid is made
opaque so that we can get a better view of

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:06.950
the waves.

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:11.700
Of course, the final simulations that we get
for production use are transparent, like the

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:13.299
one you see here.

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:19.250
Now, another interesting detail is that that
the execution time is linear with respect

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:20.989
to the curve points.

00:02:20.989 --> 00:02:22.780
So what does that mean?

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.260
Well, let’s have a look together.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:31.370
In the first scenario, we get a low-quality
underlying simulation, and we add a 100 thousand

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:32.370
wave curves.

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:37.930
This takes approximately 10 seconds and looks
like this.

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:43.410
This already greatly enhanced the quality
of the results, but we can decide to add more.

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:49.249
So, first case, a 100k wave curves, in 10-ish
seconds.

00:02:49.249 --> 00:02:54.480
Now comes the linear part - if we decide that
we are yearning for a little more, we can

00:02:54.480 --> 00:03:01.019
run 200k wave curves, and the execution time
will be 20-ish seconds.

00:03:01.019 --> 00:03:02.590
It looks like this.

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:05.030
Better, we’re getting there!

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:13.629
And for a 400k wave curves, 40-ish seconds,
and for 800k curves, yes, you guessed it right,

00:03:13.629 --> 00:03:14.989
80-ish seconds.

00:03:14.989 --> 00:03:18.159
Double the number of curves, double the execution
time.

00:03:18.159 --> 00:03:21.140
This is what the linear scaling part means.

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:24.599
Now, of course, not even this technique is
perfect.

00:03:24.599 --> 00:03:29.730
The post-processing nature of the method means
that it can enrich the underlying simulation

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:34.261
a great deal, but, it cannot add changes that
are too intrusive to it.

00:03:34.261 --> 00:03:39.680
It can only add small waves relative to the
size of the fluid domain.

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:45.389
But even with these, the value proposition
of this paper is just out of this world.

00:03:45.389 --> 00:03:51.340
So, from now on, if we have a relatively poor
quality fluid simulation that we abandoned

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:54.150
years ago, we don’t need to despair.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:04:59.099
What we need is to harness the power of wave
curves.

00:04:59.099 --> 00:05:01.650
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

